Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Redesigned strategy: keep unconstrained generation simple and conservative,
// but once a constrained arithmetic span is entered, prioritize TERMINATION and
// syntactic well-formedness over elaborate in-span steering.
//
// The main failure mode of the broken revision was runaway constrained spans:
// it kept appending parser-valid tokens for many steps and often never reached
// a complete prefix before the token budget ended. To address that root cause,
// this strategy uses a bounded in-span policy. Outside a span it performs
// ordinary unconstrained single-token generation and only reacts to the exact
// delimiter token `"<<"`, avoiding accidental synthetic openings caused by
// substring heuristics. Inside a span, it first closes immediately whenever the
// parser already reports completeness. Otherwise it performs a plain
// ConstrainedStep for the first few constrained tokens to let the arithmetic
// expression form naturally, and after that switches to a direct
// ConstrainedSample-based policy with a strong EOS penalty so decoding prefers
// continuing toward a complete arithmetic expression rather than terminating the
// whole answer early. If the constrained span grows too long without becoming
// complete, the strategy performs a verified rollback to a shorter valid prefix,
// trims generated to match, and spends one step on recovery; this prevents the
// long unterminated spans seen in the failed traces.
//
// validTokenGroups are used only as a mild bias in the early part of a span,
// where they can help arithmetic-token selection without risking the runaway
// behavior caused by overcommitting to long constrained continuations.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
